  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe provided initial solution achieves a C_bound of 0.380886, slightly higher than the target of 0.380876. Our approach uses the matched filter method from signal processing, which penalizes sharp transitions in the correlation function. This method optimizes frequency-domain components that minimize correlation across lags. Unlike the previous optimization, we use a spectral gradient method that decomposes the problem into orthogonal coordinates. The core innovation is the alternating spectral-projection technique that enforces integral constraints within the optimization loop, preventing infeasible points even during aggressive iterations. This addresses the bottleneck where local minima were trapped by strict integral constraints in the previous implementation.\n\nMathematical specification\n1. Represent h as samples of a function on [0, 2] with dx=2/n\n2. Fourier coefficients: \u0124(k) = \u03a3 h_i exp(-2\u03c0i k i / n) / \u221an\n3. Objective transformation: minimize max_k |\u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx| subjected to \u222b h(x)dx=1\n4. Functional equivalence: grid-discrete form using correlation with temperature regularization\n5. Enforcement: projection onto infeasible points using M\u00fcnch\u2013Zorn algorithm\n6. Constraints verified: sum(h) integer/enforcement by projective rearrangement\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. FourierStorage(x): \n   - Input: ndarray with n samples\n   - Output: \u0124(k) = ifft(exp[2\u03c0i k i / n]) / \u221an\n2. PenaltyFunction(\u0125):\n   - lj = |\u0125(j)| for each frequency\n   - return (max(lj) - 1)^2 + \u03b5 * (sum(lj^2)/N - \u03bc)\n3. Projection(x):\n   - Center to mean=0.5 if sum(x) inadequate\n   - Apply minimal norm adjustment within [0,1]\n4. BaseOptimization:\n   - Input: x0 (initial), n (resolution)\n   - y = ifft(fft(x0)^2)\n   - Transform to frequency space\n   - Apply penalty \u0108(\u0125) to min/max components\n   - Return gradient projection and satisfy sum(y)\u2248n/2\u22481/\u03b5\n   - Uses conjugate gradient with spectral damping\n   - Cutoff when min objective improvement per iteration <1e-6\n\nExplicit search choices:\n1. Initial candidates: \n   - Start at parent resolution 280 with stochastic perturbations\n   - Use piecewise quadratic smoothing for other resolutions\n2. Parameters:\n   - Min iterations: 12000 (tunable)\n   - Temperature: 0.01-0.005 adaptive cooling\n   - Frequency sharpening factor \u03b1=0.95\n   - Cutoff if improvement rate stagnates below 1e-8\n   - Max resolution: 600 samples (constrained by BFS within budget)\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: O(n^2) operations per gradient step at peaks, total < 10^6 ops within budget\n- Schedule checkpoints at iterations 1e4, 5e4, 8e4\n- Failsafe validation: \n  1. Compute integral via ifft(centered)\n  2. Cross-verify correlation using mode='ortho'\n  3. Fallback projection if infeasible in 2/3 trials\n- Repair mechanism: sum adjustment using discrete gradient orthogonality\n\nYour final return must include:\n1. h_values (numpy array) of length n\n2. c5_bound (float) to 8 decimal places for record\n3. n_points (int) the resolution used\n4. Method name \"SpectralProjection\" to identify solution\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Internal checkpoints: \n  validate_feasibility(h): \n    assert(mean(h)\u22480.5 and all entries [0,1])\n    recompute integral via continuity\n- External verification: \n  c = compute_c5(h)\n  assert(np.isclose(c, final_metric, rtol=1e-5))\n  Return using specified return structure",
